findings.body:
The HPEXPIREAT argv is declared with 5 slots, but the code writes `argv[5]` and passes `argc = 6` to `alsoPropagate`:

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2313"}

`argv[5] = c->argv[2]` writes past the end of the array. That is undefined behaviour: it can overwrite saved registers or other stack locals, and an ASAN build will abort here. `alsoPropagate` then reads that same out-of-bounds slot. It only works today if the compiler happens to leave padding there. Every HINCRBYFLOAT on a field with a TTL hits this path.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2306"}
        robj *argv[6];

`argv[2] = createStringObjectFromLongLong(expireat)` creates an object with refcount 1. `alsoPropagate` calls `incrRefCount` on each argv entry it stores, so the caller still owns its own reference and has to release it. This code never calls `decrRefCount`, so every HINCRBYFLOAT on a field with a TTL leaks one robj on the master.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2309-2313"}

Compare `propagateHashFieldDeletion` in the same file: it creates its argv objects, calls `alsoPropagate`, then `decrRefCount`s them.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2313-2313"}
        alsoPropagate(c->db->id, argv, 6, PROPAGATE_AOF|PROPAGATE_REPL);
        decrRefCount(argv[2]);

This fix didn't need any change to how expiry is decided, but the PR slipped one into `hashTypeGetValue`:

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="742-743"}

Before, a field whose expire time equals `commandTimeSnapshot()` counted as valid. Now it counts as expired, and on a master it is lazily deleted, with an HDEL propagated. The rest of the HFE code still uses the old rule:

::code{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2936-2940"}

`hfieldIsExpired()`, the listpack check at line 574 and the iterator check at line 1424 all use `expire < now`. So HGET/HEXISTS/HSTRLEN/HINCRBY now expire a field one millisecond earlier than HGETALL, HRANDFIELD and active expiry do. Within a single command time snapshot, a field can be visible to one command and deleted by another. Revert this unrelated change.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="742-742"}
    if (*expiredAt >= (uint64_t) commandTimeSnapshot())

`int unused_flag = 0;` is declared and never used. With `-Wall`, gcc and clang will emit an unused-variable warning, and Redis CI builds with `-Werror` in some configurations.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2242-2244"}

Remove it.

The PR re-indents the continuation line and moves the opening brace onto its own line. The function previously used K&R style with aligned parameters, like the rest of t_hash.c. Only the new parameter needed to change.

